+- E79 RF switch: DIO5/DIO6.
+- ROM bootloader backdoor: `BOOT/DIO15`, active low.
+
+Seven hardware-tested RF profiles are included:
+
+| Profile | Rate | Mode |
+| --- | ---: | --- |
+| `GFSK4K8` | 4.8 kbps | narrowband 2-GFSK |
+| `GFSK50` | 50 kbps | 2-GFSK, default |
+| `GFSK200` | 200 kbps | 2-GFSK |
+| `SLR2K5` | 2.5 kbps | SimpleLink Long Range |
+| `SLR5` | 5 kbps | SimpleLink Long Range |
+| `OOK4K8` | 4.8 kbps | OOK |
+| `IEEE154G50` | 50 kbps | IEEE 802.15.4g MR-FSK |
+
+`IEEE154G50` uses TI advanced RF commands, a 16-bit PHR, whitening, CRC-16,
+LSB-first payload bytes, and the standard 24-bit SFD `0x55904E`.
+
+The complete command reference and RF framing details are in
+`firmware/e79_at_modem/README.md`.
+
+## Hardware test
+
+Run the rigorous two-module suite:
+
+```powershell
+powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\test-e79-at-rigorous-two-modules.ps1 -PortA COM19 -PortB COM22 -BridgeBaud 1000000
+```
+
+Latest validated result:
+
+```text
+PASS: 343
+FAIL: 0
+```
+
+The suite covers AT validation, transparent and explicit TX, binary payloads,
+all seven profiles in both directions, automatic RX, sleep/wake, reset, and
+the IEEE 802.15.4g advanced command path.
+
+## GitHub Release assets
+
+Useful release assets are the `.bin`, `.hex`, `.out`, and `.map` files plus a
+`SHA256SUMS.txt` manifest. The full repository also retains these artifacts in
+the workspace for offline recovery.
+
+## ROM bootloader and J-Link recovery
+
+The CC1352P ROM serial bootloader is factory silicon code. This firmware
+configures the CCFG backdoor as:
+
+```text
+BL_CONFIG = 0xC5FE0FC5
+```
+
+That enables bootloader entry on `DIO15`, active low. A module with unknown
+CCFG or a firmware image that disables the backdoor may require J-Link:
+
+```powershell
+powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\flash-e79-at-modem.ps1
+```
+
+Physical bootloader access can permit flash read or erase operations. Revisit
+the backdoor policy before locking a production image.
